domain      = "brand"
description = "Writes a website's brand as data - a DTCG token file and a manifest - from the facts a script pulled off the site and the contract the page is painted from."

# The brand producer. Its two inputs are both text a script wrote: the brand
# contract (`make briefs` renders it from the theme's own table, so the method
# and the build cannot disagree on a token's name) and the site facts
# (`scripts/extract-site-facts.mjs`, what the site declares, verbatim and by
# frequency). The method's part is the judgment the facts do not carry: which
# colour is the accent and which the canvas, what the light mode of a
# dark-only site is, which image is the logo and which canvas it belongs on.
#
# Its output is Text carrying one JSON object with the two files, rather than
# a typed structure: a DTCG token file keys its members with `$value` and
# `$extensions` and mixes aliases with colour objects, which a structure cannot
# state and which the harness validates far more exactly than a schema on the
# output could. The harness (scripts/generate-brand.mjs) splits the object,
# validates both files against the contract, hands the problems back for a
# bounded number of repair rounds, and records every round.
#
# The model is pinned, and the phase compares nothing: one path, proved. The
# harness takes MODEL=<id> to override the pin and records which model
# produced each brand.
#
# The optional `repair` is the previous round's answer with the validator's
# problems; absent on the first round, and the guarded reference prints
# nothing.

[pipe.tokens_from_site]
type = "PipeLLM"
description = "Write a website's brand as the two data files the contract describes, from the facts pulled off the site: tokens for both modes, and the manifest."
inputs = { contract = "Text", site_facts = "Text", repair = "Text?" }
output = "Text"
# Low temperature: this is a reading of evidence, not a design. The output cap
# is well above a token file's size.
model = { model = "claude-4.8-opus", temperature = 0.2, max_tokens = 16384 }
prompt = """
You are writing a website's brand as data, for a page that will be painted from that data and from nothing else. What the site looks like has to be carried by the values you write, or it is lost.

@contract

---

Below are the FACTS a script pulled off the site: how it declares its colour scheme, the colour custom properties its stylesheets set (each with `asServed`, the value that wins for the page as it is served, by the cascade - when a framework's stylesheet lists a whole palette and the site overrides one entry, `asServed` is the site's choice - and every declaration with the selector it is set under, because a site with two schemes sets a name twice and the value under the other scheme's selector is its other mode), the colour utilities its markup uses most (with the colour each resolves to, when the stylesheet says), the colour literals its stylesheets use most, its typefaces and font links, the radii it uses, and the images that could be its logo. They are evidence, not a palette. Your judgment is the mapping: which colour is the accent, which is the canvas, which is the ink, which is the hairline, which is a raised surface. The accent is the colour the site actually shows on its links, buttons and highlights, and it may be neutral - a monochrome site has a grey accent, and a colour the page does not show is never its accent, however often a framework's stylesheet declares it: when `asServed` says a value is not what the page shows, that value is not the site's; what the OTHER mode should be when the site has one (invert the canvas and the ink, keep the accent, keep every surface a step off the canvas in the same direction, and keep the two contrast pairs above the bar); which image is the logo, and which canvas each variant belongs on - when the site offers one logo file only, use it for the canvas it was drawn for and, for the other canvas, the same file. The most frequent utilities say what the site's text and hairlines actually are; the custom properties say what the site calls its colours; the body class and the html class say which scheme it starts in. The facts may carry `stated`: what the person who owns the site said beside its URL - the accent, per mode (`stated.accent.light`, `stated.accent.dark`), and a logo for a canvas the site draws none for. A stated fact outranks every reading: `color.primary` is the stated accent in each mode it is stated for - its $$value is `stated.accent.light` and its $$extensions.mode.dark is `stated.accent.dark` - and the manifest carries the stated logo for its canvas.

@site_facts

---

@?repair

---

Write the two files as ONE JSON object: {"brand": <the manifest, brand.json>, "tokens": <the token file, tokens.json>}. Every colour as an object with sRGB components in 0..1 (never a string, except an alias "{color.<name>}"), every token with its $$description saying where on the site the value comes from, every colour token whose value is a colour object with $$value (light) and $$extensions.mode.dark (dark), an alias with $$value alone unless its dark mode differs. Output only that JSON object - no prose before or after, no code fence.
"""
